1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:05,275
The Strip of Malaysia That Ran
Through Singapore Until 2011

2
00:00:05,275 --> 00:00:09,278
Until the middle of 2011,
catching the train to Kuala Lumpur

3
00:00:09,358 --> 00:00:13,089
from the centre of Singapore
involved something strange:

4
00:00:13,169 --> 00:00:17,512
you walked into Tanjong Pagar
Railway Station, a few minutes from

5
00:00:17,592 --> 00:00:21,675
the banks of Shenton Way, and
cleared Malaysian immigration.

6
00:00:21,675 --> 00:00:26,100
The station, its platforms and
a ribbon of land running most of

7
00:00:26,180 --> 00:00:30,900
the length of the island were owned
and run by Malaysia's railway.

8
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:34,747
For forty-six years after
Singapore left the Federation,

9
00:00:34,827 --> 00:00:39,875
a strip of foreign-administered ground
cut straight through the country.

10
00:00:39,875 --> 00:00:42,788
The railway reached Singapore in 1903,

11
00:00:42,868 --> 00:00:47,750
running down from the Johor
Strait to a terminus at Tank Road.

12
00:00:47,750 --> 00:00:51,776
In 1918 the colonial
government transferred the

13
00:00:51,856 --> 00:00:56,145
railway and about 217
hectares of land along it to

14
00:00:56,225 --> 00:01:00,164
the Federated Malay
States Railways on a lease

15
00:01:00,244 --> 00:01:04,525
of 999 years, to be used
only for running trains.

16
00:01:04,525 --> 00:01:09,577
In 1932 the line into town was
rerouted, and a new terminus

17
00:01:09,657 --> 00:01:14,875
opened at Tanjong Pagar, on
reclaimed land facing the docks.

18
00:01:14,875 --> 00:01:17,286
The new station was an Art Deco

19
00:01:17,366 --> 00:01:20,500
building by the firm Swan and Maclaren.

20
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:24,512
Four marble figures over the
entrance stood for Agriculture,

21
00:01:24,592 --> 00:01:28,673
Commerce, Industry and Transport,
the initials "F M S R" were

22
00:01:28,753 --> 00:01:32,492
set across the front, and
inside the barrel-vaulted main

23
00:01:32,572 --> 00:01:37,198
hall a set of Doulton ceramic panels
showed the industries of Malaya:

24
00:01:37,278 --> 00:01:40,825
rubber tapping, tin mining,
rice planting, shipping.

25
00:01:40,825 --> 00:01:45,059
Governor Sir Cecil Clementi
opened it on the 2nd of May 1932,

26
00:01:45,139 --> 00:01:48,949
telling the crowd he had "not
the slightest doubt that,

27
00:01:49,029 --> 00:01:54,050
for centuries, this Singapore terminal
station" would serve the region.

28
00:01:54,050 --> 00:01:57,746
When Singapore separated
from Malaysia in 1965,

29
00:01:57,826 --> 00:02:02,325
the Separation Agreement said
nothing about the railway.

30
00:02:02,325 --> 00:02:06,978
The successor to the FMSR,
Malaysia's Keretapi Tanah Melayu,

31
00:02:07,058 --> 00:02:10,134
simply kept what the 1918 lease gave it:

32
00:02:10,214 --> 00:02:14,000
the track, the land beside
it, and the stations.

33
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:18,258
Tanjong Pagar, by then inside
the central business district,

34
00:02:18,338 --> 00:02:20,572
stayed a Malaysian-run terminus,

35
00:02:20,652 --> 00:02:25,199
flying the Malaysian flag, staffed
by Malaysian railway workers,

36
00:02:25,279 --> 00:02:28,750
with Malaysian customs
and immigration counters.

37
00:02:28,750 --> 00:02:32,700
Most of the time this
was just a curiosity.

38
00:02:32,700 --> 00:02:36,925
The sharpest illustration
of it came from immigration.

39
00:02:36,925 --> 00:02:40,273
Under an agreement reached
in the early 1990s,

40
00:02:40,353 --> 00:02:44,969
the two countries were to place
both their checkpoints together

41
00:02:45,049 --> 00:02:48,850
at a new Woodlands Train
Checkpoint by August 1998.

42
00:02:48,850 --> 00:02:52,925
Singapore moved its checks
to Woodlands on schedule.

43
00:02:52,925 --> 00:02:57,034
Malaysia did not: it wanted its
counters inside the Woodlands

44
00:02:57,114 --> 00:02:59,918
building rather than on the open platform,

45
00:02:59,998 --> 00:03:04,450
treated the question as bound up
with the wider unresolved dispute

46
00:03:04,530 --> 00:03:08,375
over the land, and kept its
checkpoint at Tanjong Pagar.

47
00:03:08,375 --> 00:03:12,000
For the next thirteen
years the effect was this.

48
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:15,273
A passenger leaving Singapore
by train was stamped

49
00:03:15,353 --> 00:03:19,096
out of Singapore at Woodlands,
then rode more than twenty

50
00:03:19,176 --> 00:03:22,315
kilometres south to Tanjong
Pagar and back north

51
00:03:22,395 --> 00:03:25,950
again before the train
actually crossed the Causeway.

52
00:03:25,950 --> 00:03:29,653
Someone arriving from Malaysia
was admitted to Singapore

53
00:03:29,733 --> 00:03:33,097
at Woodlands but got off
in the middle of the city,

54
00:03:33,177 --> 00:03:36,825
on ground the Singapore
government did not administer.

55
00:03:36,825 --> 00:03:41,467
Singapore called Malaysia's
arrangement a breach of the agreement;

56
00:03:41,547 --> 00:03:45,125
Malaysia called Singapore's
move a unilateral one.

57
00:03:45,125 --> 00:03:49,161
On the 27th of November 1990
the two governments signed a

58
00:03:49,241 --> 00:03:53,927
document called the Points of
Agreement, initialled by Singapore's

59
00:03:54,007 --> 00:03:58,187
prime minister Lee Kuan Yew
and Malaysia's finance minister

60
00:03:58,267 --> 00:04:02,600
Daim Zainuddin on behalf of
Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad.

61
00:04:02,600 --> 00:04:05,576
KTM would give up Tanjong Pagar and

62
00:04:05,656 --> 00:04:08,800
move its Singapore operations north.

63
00:04:08,800 --> 00:04:11,870
The railway land between Bukit Timah and

64
00:04:11,950 --> 00:04:15,100
Tanjong Pagar would return to Singapore.

65
00:04:15,100 --> 00:04:19,087
Three parcels, at Tanjong
Pagar, Kranji and Woodlands,

66
00:04:19,167 --> 00:04:22,777
would be handed to a joint
company and developed,

67
00:04:22,857 --> 00:04:27,075
sixty per cent owned by
Malaysia and forty by Singapore.

68
00:04:27,075 --> 00:04:30,900
The agreement then sat
frozen for twenty years.

69
00:04:30,900 --> 00:04:34,672
The two sides could not agree
on whether it took effect

70
00:04:34,752 --> 00:04:37,543
the day it was signed, as Singapore held,

71
00:04:37,623 --> 00:04:41,825
or only once KTM had physically
moved out, as Malaysia held.

72
00:04:41,825 --> 00:04:45,167
They argued over how the
parcels should be valued,

73
00:04:45,247 --> 00:04:48,862
over development charges,
over a Malaysian request for

74
00:04:48,942 --> 00:04:52,775
additional land at Bukit
Timah, and over the checkpoint.

75
00:04:52,775 --> 00:04:57,600
Negotiating rounds through
the 1990s produced nothing.

76
00:04:57,600 --> 00:05:02,042
A broader attempt in 2001 to
settle several disputes together —

77
00:05:02,122 --> 00:05:05,488
the railway, the price
Singapore paid for water,

78
00:05:05,568 --> 00:05:08,934
the use of Malaysian
airspace, a proposed bridge

79
00:05:09,014 --> 00:05:12,675
to replace the Causeway —
collapsed the year after.

80
00:05:12,675 --> 00:05:15,190
Mahathir stepped down in 2003,

81
00:05:15,270 --> 00:05:19,250
and relations stayed
cool for years afterward.

82
00:05:19,250 --> 00:05:23,584
On the 24th of May 2010, Prime
Ministers Lee Hsien Loong

83
00:05:23,664 --> 00:05:28,000
and Najib Razak announced
they had broken the deadlock.

84
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:30,850
KTM would move its terminus to

85
00:05:30,930 --> 00:05:34,250
Woodlands by the 1st of July 2011.

86
00:05:34,250 --> 00:05:38,987
The former railway land would
be developed by a joint company,

87
00:05:39,067 --> 00:05:43,182
M+S Private Limited, sixty
per cent held by Malaysia's

88
00:05:43,262 --> 00:05:47,921
Khazanah Nasional and forty by
Singapore's Temasek Holdings —

89
00:05:48,001 --> 00:05:51,575
the same split written
into the 1990 document.

90
00:05:51,575 --> 00:05:55,225
What had changed was the
other side of the trade.

91
00:05:55,225 --> 00:05:57,876
Instead of the three original parcels,

92
00:05:57,956 --> 00:06:04,127
Malaysia would receive six: four at Marina
South, on the reclaimed downtown waterfront,

93
00:06:04,207 --> 00:06:07,225
and two at Ophir–Rochor near Kampong Glam.

94
00:06:07,225 --> 00:06:10,007
The joint company went on to build the

95
00:06:10,087 --> 00:06:13,100
Marina One and DUO developments on them.

96
00:06:13,100 --> 00:06:16,432
It was a trade of land
for land, valued to match,

97
00:06:16,512 --> 00:06:20,550
rather than a legal ruling —
although a ruling did follow.

98
00:06:20,550 --> 00:06:23,924
In 2014 an arbitration
tribunal in The Hague

99
00:06:24,004 --> 00:06:28,084
settled a leftover question
about development charges

100
00:06:28,164 --> 00:06:31,775
on the original parcels,
in Malaysia's favour.

101
00:06:31,775 --> 00:06:35,687
Thousands of people came
to Tanjong Pagar on the

102
00:06:35,767 --> 00:06:39,675
30th of June 2011 for
the final day of service.

103
00:06:39,675 --> 00:06:44,948
They bought platform tickets to keep,
photographed the split-flap departure board,

104
00:06:45,028 --> 00:06:47,900
and prised souvenir
spikes out of the track.

105
00:06:47,900 --> 00:06:52,020
The last train pulled out
shortly before ten that night,

106
00:06:52,100 --> 00:06:55,620
driven by the Sultan of
Johor, Ibrahim Iskandar,

107
00:06:55,700 --> 00:06:59,450
whose grandfather had
opened the Causeway in 1923.

108
00:06:59,450 --> 00:07:03,425
The next morning the line
south of Woodlands closed.

109
00:07:03,425 --> 00:07:06,950
Over the following year
the rails were lifted.

110
00:07:06,950 --> 00:07:11,619
Tanjong Pagar station, gazetted
a national monument that April,

111
00:07:11,699 --> 00:07:16,825
was left standing, as were the steel
truss bridges near Bukit Timah.

112
00:07:16,825 --> 00:07:21,269
The twenty-four-kilometre strip
the trains had run on became

113
00:07:21,349 --> 00:07:26,020
the Rail Corridor, a continuous
green route from Kranji down to

114
00:07:26,100 --> 00:07:30,700
Tanjong Pagar that has been
reopening in sections since 2021.

115
00:07:30,700 --> 00:07:35,740
The terminus building is used for
exhibitions and events, and is being

116
00:07:35,820 --> 00:07:40,575
worked into the future Cantonment
MRT station on the Circle Line.

117
00:07:40,575 --> 00:07:44,922
The 2010 settlement also cleared
the way for new cross-border

118
00:07:45,002 --> 00:07:48,775
rail plans that had been
entangled with the dispute.

119
00:07:48,775 --> 00:07:51,800
The larger one did not survive.

120
00:07:51,800 --> 00:07:56,753
A high-speed line from Kuala Lumpur
to a terminus at Jurong East,

121
00:07:56,833 --> 00:08:01,245
which would have cut the journey
to around ninety minutes,

122
00:08:01,325 --> 00:08:06,510
was agreed in 2016, suspended in
2018 by a new Malaysian government,

123
00:08:06,590 --> 00:08:10,075
and formally terminated
at the start of 2021.

124
00:08:10,075 --> 00:08:13,005
Malaysia paid Singapore about 103

125
00:08:13,085 --> 00:08:16,825
million dollars for costs already run up.

126
00:08:16,825 --> 00:08:19,725
The smaller one is nearly here.

127
00:08:19,725 --> 00:08:23,793
The Johor Bahru–Singapore
Rapid Transit System Link,

128
00:08:23,873 --> 00:08:28,100
a four-kilometre shuttle
between Bukit Chagar in Johor

129
00:08:28,180 --> 00:08:31,530
Bahru and a new station
at Woodlands North,

130
00:08:31,610 --> 00:08:36,875
was itself suspended in 2019 and
revived in 2020 on changed terms.

131
00:08:36,875 --> 00:08:40,176
It is due to carry passengers by

132
00:08:40,256 --> 00:08:43,425
the end of 2026 or early 2027.

133
00:08:43,425 --> 00:08:46,644
Its main feature is that
a traveller clears both

134
00:08:46,724 --> 00:08:50,767
countries' immigration once,
at the station being left from,

135
00:08:50,847 --> 00:08:54,065
before boarding — the
reverse of the arrangement

136
00:08:54,145 --> 00:08:57,650
that made Tanjong Pagar
such an oddity for so long.

137
00:08:57,650 --> 00:09:01,472
Where it fits in the bigger
story: for forty-six years a

138
00:09:01,552 --> 00:09:05,165
foreign country administered
a line of ground through

139
00:09:05,245 --> 00:09:09,137
the middle of Singapore,
because the agreement that split

140
00:09:09,217 --> 00:09:13,050
the two countries in 1965
never dealt with the railway.

141
00:09:13,050 --> 00:09:17,472
It was resolved in the end not by
a court but by a trade of land —

142
00:09:17,552 --> 00:09:22,382
and the cross-border train it took
out of downtown is now being rebuilt,

143
00:09:22,462 --> 00:09:25,600
shorter and faster, at
the edge of the island.
